## 1. Logs, Metrics, and Traces

**Logs** are individual event records. A log usually contains a timestamp, level, message, and attributes. Log volume grows mainly with traffic because more requests produce more events. Logs are best for answering: “What exactly happened?”

**Metrics** are numeric measurements over time, such as request count, latency, CPU usage, and error rate. A metric record contains a name, timestamp, value, and labels. Metric storage grows mainly with the number of label combinations (cardinality). Metrics are best for answering: “Is the system healthy?” and “When did performance change?”

**Traces** follow one request across services. A trace contains spans, and each span has a trace ID, span ID, parent ID, timestamps, duration, and attributes. Trace volume grows with traffic and the number of services a request touches. Traces are best for answering: “Which service caused this request to be slow or fail?”

A telemetry pipeline is high-throughput and write-heavy because applications continuously send logs, metrics, and traces. It is low-frequency for reads because dashboards and investigations query the stored data less often than applications write it.

## 2. Log storage models

A full-text inverted-index system indexes words and fields when logs are written. This makes writes more expensive, but arbitrary text searches are fast. High-cardinality fields create many index entries and increase storage and memory costs.

A label-index system stores logs in compressed chunks, often in object storage. At write time it mainly stores labels and compresses log chunks, so writes are cheaper. Queries with labels such as `service=payment` are fast, but searching arbitrary text can be slower because chunks must be scanned. High-cardinality labels create too many streams and small chunks, reducing efficiency.

For a platform with very high volume and mostly “this service, last 15 minutes” searches, I would choose label-indexed compressed chunks. The query has a known service label and time range, so it can efficiently select only the relevant chunks while keeping write and storage costs lower.

## 3. Pull-based vs push-based metrics

In a pull model, a collector discovers services and regularly requests metrics from each target. The collector controls the collection rate. If a target is down, the collector notices a failed scrape. Pull works well inside one network, but firewalls or private networks can prevent the collector from reaching targets. Short-lived batch jobs may finish before the next scrape.

In a push model, applications send metrics to a collector or gateway. It works well for short-lived jobs and for applications behind firewalls because the application makes the outbound connection. The application controls when data is sent. However, if an application misbehaves and sends too much data, it can overload the collector unless rate limits, queues, or gateways are used.
